<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=polio-vaccine">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polio vaccine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Polio vaccines prevent poliomyelitis and come in two types, an inactivated virus given by injection and a weakened virus given by mouth; Hilary Koprowski first tested a live, attenuated oral vaccine successfully in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1950. Jonas Salk's injected vaccine was announced as effective in 1955, after a field trial involving 1.8 million children, and Albert Sabin's oral vaccine came into commercial use in 1961. Since a global eradication effort began in 1988, cases have fallen from an estimated 350,000 a year to 33 in 2018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polio-vaccine.jpg" alt="Polio vaccine" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CDC / Mary Hilpertshauser · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polio_vaccine_poster.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Two kinds of polio vaccine are in use. The inactivated vaccine, IPV, is given by injection; after two doses 90% or more of recipients develop protective antibodies to all three serotypes of poliovirus, and at least 99% are immune after three. The oral vaccine, OPV, is an attenuated virus produced by passing it through nonhuman cells. It needs no sterile syringes and so suits mass campaigns, and it gives both humoral and cell-mediated immunity. Its drawback is a rare risk of vaccine-associated paralytic poliomyelitis, about one case for every 2.7 million doses of the Sabin vaccine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1948 a group led by John Enders at the Children's Hospital Boston cultivated poliovirus in human tissue in the laboratory, which made vaccine research practicable; Enders, Thomas H. Weller and Frederick C. Robbins received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physiology or Medicine in 1954. During the early 1950s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> polio cases ran above 25,000 a year, with 58,000 in 1952 and 35,000 in 1953.</p><p>Hilary Koprowski's attenuated vaccine, prepared by passing the virus through mouse brains, was first tested on 27 February 1950 on an eight-year-old boy at Letchworth Village, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, and then on 19 other children. Jonas Salk's inactivated vaccine reached the market first. Because America was committed to Salk's vaccine, Albert Sabin and Koprowski tested their live vaccines elsewhere: Sabin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, Koprowski in the Congo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In 1959 ten million children in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> received the Sabin oral vaccine, and large trials there by Mikhail Chumakov and colleagues showed its safety and efficacy. The Sabin strains were chosen for worldwide distribution, and a trivalent oral vaccine was licensed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1963. Between 1962 and 1965 about 100 million Americans received it.</p><p>A global eradication effort led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-health-organization">World Health Organization</a>, UNICEF and the Rotary Foundation began in 1988 and relied largely on the Sabin-Chumakov oral vaccine. Polio was eliminated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> by 1994, in the Western Pacific in 2000 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 2002, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was declared polio-free in March 2014. The vaccine is on the WHO's List of Essential Medicines.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polio vaccine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1950</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>New York</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Hilary Koprowski · Jonas Salk · Albert Sabin · <a href="/empire/united-states">United States</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Jonas Salk’s injected vaccine was shown effective in 1955; earlier vaccines of 1934–1935 were withdrawn.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox-vaccine">Vaccination<span>1796</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=polio-vaccine">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
